<sup>1</sup>
</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><collab>on behalf of the Dutch Head and Neck Society</collab></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label><sup>1</sup></label>Radboud University Medical Center, Department of Radiation Oncology, Nijmegen, the <country>Netherlands</country></aff><aff id="aff2"><label><sup>2</sup></label>University Medical Center Utrecht, Department of Radiation Oncology, Utrecht, the <country>Netherlands</country></aff><aff id="aff3"><label><sup>3</sup></label>Department of Radiation Oncology, GROW School for Oncology and Reproduction, Maastricht University, Maastricht, the <country>Netherlands</country></aff><aff id="aff4"><label><sup>4</sup></label>Radiotherapiegroep, Department of Radiation Oncology, Arnhem, the <country>Netherlands</country></aff><aff id="aff5"><label><sup>5</sup></label>Amsterdam UMC, Department of Radiation Oncology, Amsterdam, the <country>Netherlands</country></aff><aff id="aff6"><label><sup>6</sup></label>Radboud University Medical Center, Department of Medical Imaging, Nijmegen, the <country>Netherlands</country></aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">Sven van den Bosch, MD, PhD; e-mail: <email>sven.vandenbosch@radboudumc.nl</email>.</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>10</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>15</day><month>4</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>15</day><month>4</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><!--PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on the <pub-date pub-type="epub"/>.--><volume>43</volume><issue>23</issue><fpage>2583</fpage><lpage>2594</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>3</day><month>10</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>16</day><month>1</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>4</day><month>3</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 by American Society of Clinical Oncology</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><copyright-holder>American Society of Clinical Oncology</copyright-holder><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>Creative Commons Attribution Non-Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 License: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link></license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="jco-43-2583.pdf"/><related-object source-id-type="registry-name" document-id="NCT02442375" document-id-type="clinical-trial-number" source-type="clinical-trials-registry" source-id="ClinicalTrials.gov" id="d67e206"/><abstract><sec><title>PURPOSE</title><p>Definitive radiotherapy (RT) for head and neck cancer (HNC) has significant long-term toxicity with elective neck irradiation (ENI) as a major contributor. In this multicenter randomized trial, the clinical benefit and safety of definitive RT with reduced versus standard elective dose were compared. </p></sec><sec><title>METHODS</title><p>Newly diagnosed patients with cT2-4N0-2M0 HNC were accrued and treated in five Dutch centers (definitive accelerated RT, 68 Gy in 34 fractions in 5.5 weeks). Patients receiving concurrent chemotherapy were not eligible. Dose for ENI was randomly assigned (2:1; dose reduction, 43 Gy, versus control, 50 Gy). The primary outcome was normalcy of diet score at 1 year. The secondary outcome was recurrence in electively irradiated nodes at 2 years in the dose reduction group with the null hypothesis rejected if the upper-bound one-sided 95% CI exceeded 9%. </p></sec><sec><title>RESULTS</title><p>Between 2016 and 2022, 300 patients were randomnly assigned, of whom 295 were evaluable and included in analysis (dose reduction, 196 and control, 99). The mean normalcy of diet score at 1 year was 91.6 (95% CI, 88.5 to 94.7) in the dose reduction group and 92.6 (95% CI, 88.2 to 97.1) in the control group (mean difference, &#x02013;1.1 [95% CI, &#x02013;6.5 to 4.4]). The 2-year recurrence rate in electively irradiated nodes in the dose reduction group was 4.9% (upper-bound one-sided 95% CI, 7.5%). In the control group, this was 4.3% (upper bound one-sided 95% CI, 7.7%). Exploratory analyses demonstrated less acute dysphagia grade &#x02265;3 and better xerostomia-related quality of life in the dose reduction group.</p></sec><sec><title>CONCLUSION</title><p>This is the second randomized controlled trial demonstrating that reduced elective dose is safe in definitive RT for HNC.</p></sec></abstract><counts><fig-count count="4"/><table-count count="3"/><equation-count count="0"/><ref-count count="27"/><page-count count="13"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>OPEN-ACCESS</meta-name><meta-value>TRUE</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="introduction"><title>INTRODUCTION</title><p>Definitive (chemo)radiotherapy (RT) is the treatment of choice in most patients with oropharyngeal, laryngeal, or hypopharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) when pursuing organ preservation.<sup><xref rid="b1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup> The current dose prescription practice generally consists of two dose levels. A high boost dose of 70 Gy is delivered to macroscopic tumor (ie, primary tumor and nodal metastases) and an elective dose of 50-56 Gy is delivered to the draining lymph node (LN) regions. The aim of elective neck irradiation (ENI) is to eradicate LN metastases that are not detectable by pretreatment imaging techniques. This two-dose-level concept was empirically developed during the 1960s and remained largely unchanged since.<sup><xref rid="b2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref></sup> At that time, assessment of the neck was based on palpation, and LN metastases could be easily missed. Nowadays, multimodal diagnostic imaging enables the detection of much smaller LN metastases. This has consequences for the tumor load in RT target volumes and requires adaptation of the dose prescribed.<sup><xref rid="b3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="b4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref></sup> First, a part of the LN metastases that were missed and treated with the elective dose in the past are now above the diagnostic detection threshold and are treated with the boost dose of 70 Gy. However, radiobiological models indicate that an <italic toggle="yes">intermediate dose</italic> of 60 Gy should be adequate to sterilize LNs with limited macroscopic tumor volume.<sup><xref rid="b3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="b5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup> Second, the tumor load of LN metastases that are missed with modern diagnostic work-up has decreased as a result of improved detection. However, the elective dose of 50-56 Gy has remained unchanged while radiobiological models indicate that a <italic toggle="yes">de-escalated dose</italic> of 34-40 Gy should be sufficient to sterilize LNs from current subclinical tumor volume.<sup><xref rid="b3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="b5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup> Given these developments, there is a window of opportunity for dose reduction to LNs while maintaining treatment efficacy.<sup><xref rid="b3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="b4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref></sup> Because ENI contributes to dysphagia and xerostomia in a dose-dependent way, this approach is expected to reduce toxicity and to better preserve quality of life (QoL).<sup><xref rid="b6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="b7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref></sup> The UPGRADE-RT trial was conducted to compare the clinical benefit and safety of definitive accelerated RT alone with reduced versus standard elective dose to LNs in patients with oropharyngeal, laryngeal, and hypopharyngeal SCC.</p><boxed-text id="B1" position="anchor"><sec sec-type="context-box"><title>CONTEXT</title><p>
<list list-type="simple"><list-item><p>
<bold>Key Objective</bold>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Is definitive radiotherapy (RT) alone (68 Gy in 34 fractions in 5.5 weeks) with reduced (43 Gy) versus standard (50 Gy) dose to elective nodal regions safe with less toxicity in patients with head and neck cancer?</p></list-item><list-item><p>
<bold>Knowledge Generated</bold>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>The 2-year recurrence rate in electively irradiated nodes in the dose reduction group was 4.9% (upper bound one-sided 95% CI, 7.5%), which was less than the prespecified boundary for acceptability (9%). With standard dose this was 4.3% (upper bound one-sided 95% CI, 7.7%). The normalcy of diet was not different between groups, but nearly 90% of patients had unrestricted full diet.</p></list-item><list-item><p>
<bold>Relevance <italic toggle="yes">(M.L. Gillison)</italic></bold>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>For patients who meet the study eligibility criteria, a reduction of RT dose to elective lymph node regions from 50 to 43 Gy is associated with minimal change to regional recurrence rates and diet at 1 year; longer-term follow up may be required to determine effect on chronic toxicities of therapy. The reduced dose can be considered an option for standard of care.*</p></list-item><list-item><p>*Relevance section written by <italic toggle="yes">JCO</italic> Associate Editor Maura L. Gillison, MD, PhD.</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec></boxed-text></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>METHODS</title><sec><title>Study Design and Participants</title><p>The UPGRADE-RT trial is an investigator-initiated, multicenter, single-blind, randomized controlled trial (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: <ext-link xlink:href="https://www.clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT02442375" ext-link-type="uri">NCT02442375</ext-link>). Patients were consented, enrolled, and treated at five Head and Neck Oncologic Centers in the Netherlands. The study protocol was approved by the Medical Research Ethics Committee at the Radboud University Medical Center (Nijmegen, the Netherlands).<sup><xref rid="b8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref></sup> Eligible patients had newly diagnosed stage cT2-4N0-2M0 SCC of the oropharynx, larynx, or hypopharynx (American Joint Committee on Cancer 7th edition) with decision for definitive locoregional accelerated RT alone (no concurrent chemotherapy). Ineligibility criteria were concurrent systemic treatment, previous anticancer treatment to the head and neck area, or a history of invasive malignancies within the last 3 years. Pretreatment evaluation included flexible endoscopy of the upper aerodigestive tract, computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance imaging, histological tumor biopsy, and ultrasound of the neck with fine needle aspirated cytology. Results of diagnostic work-up, staging, and treatment recommendation were discussed in a multidisciplinary conference. For patients with locally advanced disease, decision was made for definitive accelerated RT alone when unfit for concurrent chemotherapy (age &#x02265;70 years or comorbidities) or cT3N0-1 laryngeal cancers (following European consensus guidelines).<sup><xref rid="b1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup> Participants were randomly assigned in a 2:1 ratio to receive RT with reduced (dose reduction group) or standard (control group) elective dose using minimization.<sup><xref rid="b9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="b10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup> Minimization is a widely accepted method that may be preferred over stratified randomization in trials with multiple variables to be balanced for and when the sample size is moderate.<sup><xref rid="b10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup> Variables balanced for were institution, tumor site, T-classification, N-classification, and human papilloma virus status for oropharyngeal cancers. In minimization, the first participant is truly randomly allocated. For each subsequent participant, the treatment allocation that minimizes the imbalance on the selected variables between groups at that time is identified. Weighted randomization was used to ensure an 80% chance for allocation to the group that minimizes imbalance. Participants were masked for treatment allocation.</p></sec><sec><title>Radiation Treatment</title><p>For radiation treatment planning, iodinated contrast-enhanced [18F]-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) positron emission tomography (PET)/CT scans of the head and neck area were acquired in radiation treatment position. Image acquisition was performed on EARL<sup><xref rid="b11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref></sup>-accredited PET/CT scanners following European guidelines.<sup><xref rid="b12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref></sup> Participants were treated with external beam RT with intensity modulation and simultaneous integrated boost. The dose fractionation scheme used in the first 38 (of 300) participants was 66 Gy in 33 fractions delivered in 5 weeks. Because late laryngeal toxicity was higher than expected, the protocol was amended by changing fractionation to 68 Gy in 34 fractions delivered in 5.5 weeks (Data Supplement, online only).</p><p>Methods for delineation and target volume definition are described in detail in the study protocol.<sup><xref rid="b8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref></sup> The gross primary tumor volume was delineated by traditional means and a biological target volume was created by adaptive threshold iso-contouring on FDG-PET imaging.<sup><xref rid="b13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref></sup> Clinical target volumes and planning target volumes (PTV) were created to take microscopic tumor extensions and patient set-up uncertainties into account. Treatment of LNs was different between groups. <italic toggle="yes">In the dose reduction group</italic>, LNs were classified into three risk levels using a standardized risk-assessment algorithm.<sup><xref rid="b13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>,<xref rid="b14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref></sup> High-risk LNs comprised overtly metastatic LNs and were identified by (1) positive cytology or (2) central necrosis on imaging or (3) increased FDG uptake (&#x02265;2&#x000d7; background). Intermediate-risk LNs comprised LNs with limited macroscopic tumor volume and were identified by (1) summed long- and short-axis diameter &#x02265;17 mm and (2) increased FDG uptake (&#x02265;1.5 and &#x0003c;2&#x000d7; background).<sup><xref rid="b13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>,<xref rid="b14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref></sup> Low-risk LN volumes comprised the elective LN regions including all LNs not classifying as high- or intermediate-risk. The dose prescribed to high-, intermediate-, and low-risk PTVs was 68 Gy, 60 Gy, and 43 Gy, respectively. <italic toggle="yes">In the control group</italic>, no standardized risk-assessment algorithm for LNs was used. LNs were either classified high-risk or low-risk by traditional means (ie, positive cytology, short-axis diameter &#x02265;10 mm, or central necrosis). The FDG-PET scan was used in the decision- making process by means of visual interpretation. Selection and delineation of elective LN regions were performed according to international guidelines and were not different between groups.<sup><xref rid="b15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref></sup> The dose prescribed to the high- and low-risk PTVs in the control group was 68 Gy and 50 Gy. Follow-up was performed until 2 years after treatment. Assessment of disease status included physical examination and flexible endoscopy of the upper aerodigestive tract and was performed every 2 and 3 months during the first and second year after treatment.</p></sec><sec><title>Outcomes</title><p>The primary outcome was the normalcy of diet score of the clinician-rated Performance Status Scale for Head and Neck (PSS-HN) at 1 year.<sup><xref rid="b16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref></sup> This is a ranking of 10 food categories assessing the degree to which a patient is able to eat a normal diet with scores ranging from 0 (total incapacity) to 100 (normal functioning; Data Supplement). The secondary outcome was recurrence in electively irradiated LNs at 2 years. The radiation treatment plan and imaging of all regional recurrences were centrally reviewed to identify all events.</p><p>Other outcomes included acute and late toxicity, QoL, functional performance, recurrence, and survival. Acute (occurring within 90 days since start of treatment) and late toxicity (occurring &#x0003e;90 days since start of treatment) were scored using the common toxicity criteria for adverse events. QoL and functional performance were assessed at baseline and at 3, 6, 12 and 24 months using the European Organisation for Research and Treatment of Cancer (EORTC) QLQ-C30 and H&#x00026;N35 questionnaires and the PSS-HN.<sup><xref rid="b16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>-<xref rid="b18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref></sup></p></sec><sec><title>Statistical Analysis</title><p>This study was designed to detect a 10-point difference between groups in the mean normalcy of diet score at 1 year (primary outcome), assuming a standard deviation of 24 points, with a two-sided alpha of .05 and 90% power. An analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) was performed to compare the normalcy of diet scores between groups, with baseline score included as covariate. For the secondary outcome, studies with similar inclusion criteria as the current study demonstrate a 2-year recurrence rate in electively irradiated LNs of 5% with standard dose.<sup><xref rid="b14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>,<xref rid="b19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref></sup> We also expected a recurrence rate of 5% in the dose reduction group and choose an upper boundary for acceptability of 9%. This was based on European consensus guidelines recommending ENI when the risk for occult LN metastases exceeds 10-15%, implying a recurrence rate below 10% can be accepted when ENI is not performed.<sup><xref rid="b20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref></sup> On the basis of these data, our null hypothesis was that the risk of recurrence in the dose reduction group was &#x02265;9% versus the alternative hypothesis of a recurrence risk &#x0003c;9%. We proposed to reject the null hypothesis, and declare that reduced elective dose is safe, if the upper limit of the one-sided 95% CI of the 2-year recurrence rate in electively irradiated LNs was less than the boundary for acceptability of 9%. This was estimated using the Kaplan-Meier method. Time-to-event was calculated from the date of last RT and censored by death or loss to follow-up. With a one-sided alpha of .10 and 80% power, the required sample size in the dose reduction group was 180. Using an unequal randomization ratio of 2:1 (dose reduction:control) and 10% dropout rate, the total sample size needed was 300.</p><p>For other recurrence and survival outcomes and late toxicity (grade &#x02265;3) the Kaplan-Meier method was used and differences between groups were tested using log-rank tests. Rates were presented with 95% CI. Univariable Cox regression analysis was performed to estimate hazard ratios (HRs) with 95% CI. Between-group differences in acute toxicity (grade &#x02265;3) were tested using univariable logistic regression. Relative risk (RR) with 95% CI was reported to provide a more interpretable measure of association and was calculated using marginal standardization with the delta method. The EORTC QLQ-C30 and H&#x00026;N35 were analyzed using linear mixed models with treatment, time point, and their interaction as fixed effects and patient as random effect. The difference in mean scores of at least 10 points between groups was considered clinically meaningful.<sup><xref rid="b21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref></sup> Scores of the PSS-HN were compared between groups using ANCOVA with baseline score included as a covariate. The completion rate of QoL questionnaires and PSS-HN was defined as the percentage of completed measurements relative to the number expected (patients who remained in the trial and were alive). <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values &#x0003c;.05 were considered statistically significant. Statistical analyses were performed using R (Version 4.4.1, R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>RESULTS</title><p>Between July 28, 2016, and January 25, 2022, 300 patients were randomly assigned to treatment, 199 and 101 to the dose reduction and control group. One patient withdrew consent early and four went off-study due to major eligibility infringement before start of treatment, resulting in 196 and 99 evaluable patients, respectively (Fig <xref rid="fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>). All 295 were included in the intention-to-treat analysis. The final follow-up date was July 3, 2024. The median follow-up was 24.5 months (95% CI, 24.3 to 24.7). Baseline and treatment characteristics are listed in Table <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">1</xref>. Main outcome results are listed in Table <xref rid="tbl2" ref-type="table">2</xref>.</p><fig position="float" id="fig1" fig-type="figure"><label>FIG 1.</label><caption><p>CONSORT diagram of enrolled patients.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jco-43-2583-g001" position="float"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="tbl1"><label>TABLE 1.</label><caption><p>Baseline and Treatment Characteristics</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Characteristic</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Control Group (n = 99)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dose Reduction Group (n = 196)</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sex, No. (%)</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Male</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">80 (81)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">146 (74)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Female</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">19 (19)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50 (26)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age at random assignment, years, median (IQR; range)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67 (60-74; 42-90)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">66 (59-72; 41-90)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;&#x02264;59, No. (%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">23 (23)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">52 (26)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;60-69, No. (%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">33 (33)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">68 (35)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;70-79, No. (%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36 (36)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">63 (32)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;&#x02265;80, No. (%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7 (7)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13 (7)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Smoking tobacco</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Pack-years (IQR; range)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30 (14-48; 0-100)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30 (15-45; 0-156)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Never smoker, No. (%)</td>